My father, grandfather, and great-grandfather all got struck non-fatally
by lightning.  At the age of 20, I had a "near miss" within 50 yards 
myself. 

It's quite a string of luck, isn't it?  I mean, what were the odds?  
I know weather is just a natural phenomenon, but given the history, 
it sure seems personal.  Even so, I'm not counting on the next time 
being non-fatal.  

Anyway I live now in a coastal climate where the weather is calm, 
there's been maybe one violent storm in fifteen years, and lightning 
is not even visible more than once a decade.  Furthermore, we're 
surrounded here by hills.  Hills higher up than the places where I 
live and work, and close enough to ground the clouds much more easily 
than nailing anyplace I actually go would. The odds of getting the 
"big jolt" in an up-close and personal way here are so small they're 
not worth measuring, unlike the great plains where the aforementioned
incidents occurred.  I should be more worried about earthquakes. 

Even so, I do not work on fences in rainstorms, shelter from downpours
under trees, or own or use umbrellas with metal handles, these being the 
three "proximate-cause" mistakes made by my forebears.

It's not fear, as such; it's three simple rules that cost me nothing, 
observed as much out of respect for powerful natural forces and for 
the memory of my (now-deceased) forebears as for any rational reason.  

Let people have their quirks.  I understand that what happened in 
my family was a statistical fluke, even for an area with frequent 
violent storms.  I understand that most people's personal experience 
and family history of lightning doesn't match mine.  But I refuse 
to disrespect that history. 

If somebody's had a string of strange luck and now prefers to manage 
things differently, in a way that costs nothing or near it, let them.

